    "lawSection": "Alcoholic Beverage Control Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S4241A\n\nSPONSOR: KAMINSKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the alcoholic beverage control law, in\nrelation to authorizing retail licenses to purchase beer, wine or liquor\nwith a business payment card\n\n \nPURPOSE: OF THE BILL:\n\nTo allow licensed retailers to use business credit cards to purchase\nwine, wine products, beer and liquor from licensed distributors and\nwholesalers.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends S 101-aa(1) of the Alcoholic Beverage Control Law to\nestablishes definitions.\n\nSection 2 amends S 101-aa(2) of the Alcoholic Beverage Control Law to\npermit retail licensees to use business payment cards for the purchase\nof wine and liquor.\n\nSection 3 amends S 101-aaa(1) of the Alcoholic Beverage Control Law to\nestablish the definitions.\n\nSection 4 amends § 101-aaa(2) of the Alcoholic Beverage Control Law.\n\nSection 5 amends § 55-b to address the 180 day rule\n\nSection 6 establishes the effective date.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nUnder current law, retail licensees may only pay for the purchase of\nliquor, wine, beer, cider, and wine products with cash, certified check,\nmoney order, electronic funds transfer, bank officer's check or draft,\nor check.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCredit cards are basic tools of modern commerce, and are used regularly\nthroughout the world today. As such, alcohol beverage retailers should\nbe permitted to use to business credit cards to purchase products from\nduly licensed alcohol beverage wholesalers and distributors. Allowing\nlicensed retailers to buy alcohol beverages from distributors using\ncredit cards will facilitate commercial transactions and improve\naccountability through enhanced record-keeping, while preserving regula-\ntory policy objectives such as credit limitations and tied-house\nrestrictions.\n\nIf a retailer uses a credit card to buy alcohol beverage inventory, the\nwholesaler receives payment immediately. The wholesaler is not extending\ncredit to the retailer; rather, credit is extended by a financial insti-\ntution that has no direct interest in the alcohol beverage industry. The\ninvolvement of the third-party financial institution actually creates an\nadditional barrier between the retailer and the, wholesaler, effectively\nalleviating the historical concerns behind these credit limitations.\n\n\nMoreover, the involvement of the financial institution issuing the cred-\nit card increases timely and effective accountability through independ-\nent, timely and effective record-keeping as to all credit card trans-\nactions. Hence, as a matter of alcohol regulatory policy, the use of a\ncredit card in no way affords the wholesaler an opportunity to gain\nundue influence with the retailer; the ensuing credit obligation owed by\nthe retailer is to the card-issuer, not the merchant selling the alcohol\ninventory to the card holder.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2018- S7591 (Lanza)  \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect 90 days after enactment, with provisions.\n",
